WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.327
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book three.

2
00:00:05.627 --> 00:00:09.304
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.604 --> 00:00:15.112
Unit eight: Legal Theory. Today you will debate law using should + modal for debate.

4
00:00:15.412 --> 00:00:17.971
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:18.933 --> 00:00:22.259
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.117 --> 00:00:24.466
Jurisprudence.

7
00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:28.820
Jurisprudence means the philosophy and theory of law.

8
00:00:29.417 --> 00:00:32.914
Jurisprudence asks what law is and why it binds.

9
00:00:33.517 --> 00:00:36.320
Your turn. Say: Jurisprudence.

10
00:00:39.167 --> 00:00:40.458
Jurisprudence.

11
00:00:41.217 --> 00:00:42.438
Natural law.

12
00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:48.480
Natural law means the view that law is grounded in universal moral principles.

13
00:00:49.083 --> 00:00:53.115
Natural law holds that an unjust law is no true law.

14
00:00:53.717 --> 00:00:56.624
Your turn. Say: Natural law.

15
00:00:59.467 --> 00:01:00.967
Natural law.

16
00:01:01.717 --> 00:01:03.217
Legal positivism.

17
00:01:04.217 --> 00:01:09.374
Legal positivism means the view that law is whatever authority validly enacts.

18
00:01:09.983 --> 00:01:12.900
Legal positivism separates law from morality.

19
00:01:13.517 --> 00:01:16.541
Your turn. Say: Legal positivism.

20
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.016
Legal positivism.

21
00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:22.635
Precedent.

22
00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:27.020
Precedent means an earlier decision that guides later cases.

23
00:01:27.617 --> 00:01:31.671
The court followed precedent from 1954.

24
00:01:32.283 --> 00:01:35.040
Your turn. Say: Precedent.

25
00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:38.820
Precedent.

26
00:01:39.583 --> 00:01:40.654
Rule of law.

27
00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:46.262
Rule of law means the principle that everyone, including rulers, is subject to law.

28
00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:50.278
The rule of law protects citizens from arbitrary power.

29
00:01:50.883 --> 00:01:53.965
Your turn. Say: Rule of law.

30
00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:58.556
Rule of law.

31
00:01:59.317 --> 00:02:01.084
Judicial interpretation.

32
00:02:02.083 --> 00:02:06.219
Judicial interpretation means judges' explanation of what a law means.

33
00:02:06.817 --> 00:02:10.732
Judicial interpretation can extend a statute to new cases.

34
00:02:11.317 --> 00:02:14.631
Your turn. Say: Judicial interpretation.

35
00:02:17.467 --> 00:02:19.095
Judicial interpretation.

36
00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:20.990
Legal reasoning.

37
00:02:21.983 --> 00:02:25.957
Legal reasoning means the logic by which judges reach decisions.

38
00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:30.036
Legal reasoning applies general rules to particular facts.

39
00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:33.558
Your turn. Say: Legal reasoning.

40
00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:37.598
Legal reasoning.

41
00:02:38.350 --> 00:02:40.094
Statutory construction.

42
00:02:41.083 --> 00:02:44.673
Statutory construction means the rules for interpreting written laws.

43
00:02:45.283 --> 00:02:48.258
Statutory construction resolves unclear wording.

44
00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:51.909
Your turn. Say: Statutory construction.

45
00:02:54.767 --> 00:02:56.557
Statutory construction.

46
00:02:57.367 --> 00:03:01.134
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:03:01.983 --> 00:03:06.328
Visal, should a judge follow an old decision even if it now seems wrong?

48
00:03:07.417 --> 00:03:16.617
Judges should balance precedent with flexible interpretation. Stability matters, but justice should not be sacrificed to habit.

49
00:03:17.717 --> 00:03:20.401
Doesn't flexibility make law unpredictable?

50
00:03:21.517 --> 00:03:31.565
It could. That is why positivists argue that judges ought to apply the law as written, and that legislatures should change it.

51
00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:34.603
And natural law theorists?

52
00:03:35.717 --> 00:03:41.815
They would say that a grossly unjust rule should not be enforced, whatever its source.

53
00:03:42.967 --> 00:03:44.688
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:45.550 --> 00:03:48.513
Grammar focus: Should plus modal for debate.

55
00:03:48.813 --> 00:03:49.977
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:50.683 --> 00:03:54.099
Judges should balance precedent with flexible interpretation.

57
00:03:54.983 --> 00:03:58.341
Legislatures ought to amend unjust statutes.

58
00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:02.190
A grossly unjust rule should not be enforced.

59
00:04:03.133 --> 00:04:12.406
In debate use should / ought to / must + base verb to state a position. 'Should' is the most flexible and the least absolute. Introduce counterpoints with 'yet' or 'however'.

60
00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:17.103
Be careful! Don't say: Judges should to follow precedent.

61
00:04:19.067 --> 00:04:21.600
Say: Judges should follow precedent.

62
00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:23.343
No 'to' after should.

63
00:04:24.133 --> 00:04:26.820
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:27.633 --> 00:04:28.669
What Is Law?.

65
00:04:28.969 --> 00:04:33.604
Jurisprudence debates the nature of law and how judges ought to decide cases.

66
00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:41.104
Legal positivists hold that law is whatever authority validly enacts, so judges should apply it as written.

67
00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:48.291
Natural law theorists reply that a rule that grossly violates justice should not be treated as binding.

68
00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:54.771
Precedent gives stability and predictability, yet rigid adherence may perpetuate old mistakes.

69
00:04:55.767 --> 00:05:01.632
Most modern courts therefore balance precedent with careful interpretation, guided by the rule of law.

70
00:05:02.733 --> 00:05:06.700
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:07.550 --> 00:05:10.444
Judges blank balance precedent with interpretation.

72
00:05:10.744 --> 00:05:14.339
A, should. B, should to. C, shoulds.

73
00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:19.969
The answer is A: should.

74
00:05:21.117 --> 00:05:25.032
The view that law is grounded in universal moral principles is blank.

75
00:05:25.332 --> 00:05:29.659
A, natural law. B, legal positivism. C, precedent.

76
00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:35.316
The answer is A: natural law.

77
00:05:36.450 --> 00:05:39.483
An earlier decision that guides later cases is a blank.

78
00:05:39.783 --> 00:05:43.854
A, statute. B, precedent. C, jurisprudence.

79
00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:49.504
The answer is B: precedent.

80
00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:52.475
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:52.775 --> 00:05:59.355
A, Courts should respects precedent.. B, Courts should respect precedent.. C, Courts should to respect precedent..

82
00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:06.281
The answer is B: Courts should respect precedent..

83
00:06:07.417 --> 00:06:10.670
According to the reading, rigid adherence to precedent may…

84
00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:16.411
A, perpetuate old mistakes. B, ensure justice. C, end the rule of law.

85
00:06:19.967 --> 00:06:23.011
The answer is A: perpetuate old mistakes.

86
00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:27.143
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:27.983 --> 00:06:32.108
Should judges strictly follow precedent, or interpret the law more flexibly?

88
00:06:48.917 --> 00:06:50.312
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:50.612 --> 00:07:14.075
Judges should generally follow precedent, because it provides stability and equal treatment. However, they should be willing to depart from it when it is clearly unjust or outdated. Flexibility must be reasoned and transparent, not personal preference. A balanced approach protects both predictability and fairness, which are two pillars of the rule of law.

90
00:07:14.375 --> 00:07:16.410
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:17.317 --> 00:07:21.536
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and should plus modal for debate.

92
00:07:21.836 --> 00:07:26.648
Today's value is critical reasoning. Test every argument before you accept it.

93
00:07:26.948 --> 00:07:29.287
See you in Unit nine. Goodbye!

94
00:07:29.587 --> 00:07:32.216
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

